Let the password manager fit a phone.

Its right edge was cut off: the window measured 377px and the page laid out at
556. #content is a flex item, and a flex item's min-width defaults to its
min-content width, so it declined to shrink and the overflow sat on #container
as horizontal scroll nobody could reach.

The file already sets min-width on `#content *` to hold the layout open above
1300px. This is the same selector releasing the same elements below 600, which
is the only way to reach them -- a floor set by the initial value of min-width
cannot be lifted from the container.

The centred card inside is sized as a percentage of what it lands in, so once
nothing holds the width open it follows the window down.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after chromium-154-harmonyos.patch.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/resources/password_manager/password_manager_app.html b/chrome/browser/resources/password_manager/password_manager_app.html
--- a/chrome/browser/resources/password_manager/password_manager_app.html
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/password_manager/password_manager_app.html
@@ -71,6 +71,24 @@
   @media not (max-width: 1300px) {
     #content * {
       min-width: 680px;
+    }
+  }
+
+  /* A phone. #content is a flex item, and a flex item's min-width defaults to
+   * its min-content width -- 556px here -- so it refuses to shrink and the
+   * right side of the page ends up past the edge of a 377px window. The rule
+   * above does the same thing in the other direction on a wide screen, which
+   * is why the selector looks like that: it has to reach the same elements to
+   * undo their floor. The centred card is a percentage of whatever it lands
+   * in, so once nothing is holding the width open it follows the window. */
+  @media (max-width: 600px) {
+    #container {
+      overflow-x: hidden;
+    }
+
+    #content,
+    #content * {
+      min-width: 0;
     }
   }
 
